```spl
/*
===================================================================
SPL QUERY: Suspicious Process Creation & Post-Logon Triage
File: splunk/suspicious-process-analysis.spl
Target Event: Sysmon Event ID 1 (Process Create)
Sourcetypes: XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational OR _json
===================================================================

1. INVESTIGATION OBJECTIVE:
   Identify suspicious command-line executions, reconnaissance utilities (whoami, net, ipconfig),
   scripting engines launched with evasion arguments (-ExecutionPolicy Bypass, -WindowStyle Hidden),
   and inspect parent-child relationships under specific logon sessions.

2. REQUIRED FIELDS & ASSUMPTIONS:
   - EventCode / EventID: 1 (Sysmon Process Create).
   - Image: Full filesystem path of the spawned executable.
   - CommandLine: Exact arguments passed to the binary.
   - ParentImage: Binary that initiated execution.
   - ParentCommandLine: Arguments of the parent.
   - ProcessId: Operating system PID.
   - ParentProcessId: Parent PID.
   - User: Security context under which the process was executed.
   - LogonId: LSA session identifier matching Windows Security Event 4624.
   - Hashes: Cryptographic hashes (MD5, SHA256) of the binary.
*/

index=* (EventCode=1 OR EventID=1)
| eval ProcImage=coalesce(Image, process_path, process_name)
| eval ProcCmd=coalesce(CommandLine, process_exec, cmdline)
| eval ParentProc=coalesce(ParentImage, parent_process_path, parent_process_name)
| eval ProcUser=coalesce(User, user)
| eval SessionID=coalesce(LogonId, LogonGuid)
| eval HashVal=coalesce(Hashes, hash)
| eval SuspiciousScore=0
| eval SuspiciousScore=if(match(ProcCmd, "(?i)(-ExecutionPolicy\s+Bypass|-enc|-w\s+hidden|WindowStyle\s+Hidden)"), SuspiciousScore+3, SuspiciousScore)
| eval SuspiciousScore=if(match(ProcCmd, "(?i)(whoami|net\.exe|quser|Get-NetTCPConnection|ipconfig)"), SuspiciousScore+2, SuspiciousScore)
| eval SuspiciousScore=if(match(ProcCmd, "(?i)(C:\\Users\\Public\\|AppData\\Local\\Temp\\)"), SuspiciousScore+2, SuspiciousScore)
| eval IsPivotSession=if(SessionID="0x14A28F", "YES (Target Session)", "NO")
| table _time, ProcUser, SessionID, IsPivotSession, ParentProc, ProcImage, ProcCmd, ProcessId, ParentProcessId, SuspiciousScore, HashVal
| sort - _time

/*
===================================================================
3. EXPECTED OUTPUT ON SYNTHETIC DATASET:
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   _time                ProcUser  SessionID  IsPivotSession         ParentProc              ProcImage           ProcCmd                                           SuspiciousScore
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2026-10-24 14:30:02  LAB\jdoe  0x14A28F   YES (Target Session)   C:\Windows\explorer.exe C:\...\cmd.exe      cmd.exe /c dir C:\Users\jdoe\Desktop /s > C:\...  2
   2026-10-24 14:25:30  LAB\jdoe  0x14A28F   YES (Target Session)   C:\Windows\...\cmd.exe  C:\...\powershell   powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -NoProfile 5
   2026-10-24 14:23:45  LAB\jdoe  0x14A28F   YES (Target Session)   C:\Windows\explorer.exe C:\...\cmd.exe      cmd.exe /c whoami /all                            2
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. ANALYST REASONING:
   - The user session `0x14A28F` (authenticated via RDP after 2 failed attempts) immediately initiated:
     1. `whoami /all` (T1033 - System Owner/User Discovery) to inspect token privileges.
     2. A child PowerShell instance launched with `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden` querying active listening ports (`Get-NetTCPConnection`) and piping output to `C:\Users\Public\net_recon.txt`.
     3. A subsequent directory listing of the user's Desktop piped to `C:\Users\Public\file_list.txt`.
   - The combination of discovery commands, output redirection to a shared directory (`C:\Users\Public`), and command-line evasion flags elevates this activity from benign user activity to highly suspicious post-exploitation discovery.

5. POTENTIAL FALSE POSITIVES:
   - IT Administration scripts or SCCM/Intune deployment jobs running PowerShell with bypass flags.
   - Legitimate software installers writing temporary staging files to `C:\Users\Public`.
   - Security auditing or compliance tools running inventory scripts.
===================================================================
*/
```
